    desc: 'Ethiopian crosses — also called Abyssinian or Ethiopian-Eritrean crosses — are distinguished by elaborate openwork latticework whose intertwined pattern represents everlasting life, and by the rule that no two crosses are identical: artisans exercise individual taste in shape and pattern. Processional types carry a socket at the base for mounting on a staff to be carried in church ceremonies; priests bless with smaller hand-held crosses. Cast, chased and incised in brass, bronze, iron and wood, they descend from Aksumite Christian metalwork; the celebrated Lalibela Cross is among the most famous, and crosses remain central to liturgy and Timkat festivals.',
