    desc: 'In the capital of the Kingdom of Aksum, single quarried stones of phonolite were carved into multi-storey towers: the 4th-century CE Obelisk of Axum rises 24 meters and weighs 160 tonnes, ornamented with two false doors at the base and window-like decoration on all faces, ending in a semi-circular top once enclosed by metal frames. The even larger Great Stele fell, likely in antiquity, while King Ezana\'s stele still stands. Stelae marked royal burial complexes and translated palace facades into stone; the Obelisk, taken to Rome in 1937, was returned and re-erected at Aksum in 2008 within the UNESCO World Heritage site.',
