    desc: 'Ethiopian monasteries have copied and painted Ge\'ez gospel codices on goatskin parchment for some 1,600 years. The Garima Gospels of Tigray — three manuscripts whose earliest radiocarbon dates run to c. 390-570 — include what is believed to be the earliest surviving complete illuminated Christian gospel book, its canon tables and portraits linked to late antique Coptic Egypt, Nubia and Himyar. Imperial Gondar, founded in 1636, fostered the Gondarine style: diptychs, triptychs, murals and illuminated manuscripts with religious motifs, patronized alongside 44 churches around Lake Tana. Monastic scriptoria bind hand-sewn wooden-board codices to this day.',
