    desc: 'The coral-stone houses of Stone Town are fronted by monumental carved doors, a Swahili signature born of Arab, Persian, Indian, European and African traditions mixing. Two types dominate: the square-panelled door with brass studs associated with the Indian subcontinent, and the older coastal type with deep relief around a central carved post. Symbolic carving — lotus flowers, fish, date palms and hanging chains — carries wishes of fertility, abundance and security, while Arabic inscriptions bless the household. Carvers, many tracing to Gujarat, served Omani merchants; the Metropolitan Museum holds a Swahili carved door of the mid- to late 19th century.',
