    desc: 'Imigongo is made by women who mix cow dung with ash — which kills bacteria and removes odor — and mold it on walls or boards into ridged spirals, zigzags and diamonds that harden before painting in organic colors: traditionally black, white, red, grey and beige-yellow. First found inside Kibungo hut walls in the 18th century as "magical" decoration, legend credits Prince Kakira of Gisaka with inventing it in the 1800s. The 1994 genocide nearly erased the skills; a women\'s cooperative on the road to Rusumo near Kirehe revived them, and artists now experiment with modern images of the Rwandan landscape alongside the classic geometry.',
