    sum: 'Zimbabwe\'s celebrated modern stone-carving movement — serpentine spirits, family groups and metamorphoses by first-generation masters like Mukomberanwa and Munyaradzi.',
    desc: 'Stone sculpture, and stone carving in particular, is the art for which Zimbabwe is known worldwide. From the late 1950s, pioneers such as Joram Mariga at Nyanga and communities like the Tengenenge sculptor village, encouraged by National Gallery director Frank McEwen, developed direct carving of local serpentine, springstone and soapstone — the term "Shona sculpture" was McEwen\'s, though artists of several peoples joined. First-generation masters including Nicholas Mukomberanwa, Henry Munyaradzi, Bernard Takawira and Colleen Madamombe carved spirits, mother-and-child groups and animal metamorphoses, exhibited internationally from the 1970s and renewing Great Zimbabwe\'s stone heritage in a modern personal voice.',
