    desc: 'Xibelani (also Shibelani) is the indigenous dance of Tsonga women of Limpopo and Mpumalanga; the name comes from Xitsonga and translates roughly as "hitting to the rhythm", while the skirt itself is the tinguvu. Its roots reach to early coastal southern Mozambique of the 1400s or earlier, where Chopi timbila orchestras — mbila xylophones and swigubu drums — accompanied women singing call-and-response between claps and whistles. Dancers shake the hips so the whole skirt rattles: old xibelani of fibers, wool, strings and grass, modern ones of strong pleated cloth. The dance remains central to Tsonga community celebration and identity.',
